@@ -0,0 +1,232 @@
+# Persistent realms and explicit host objects
+
+Status: initial realm extension implemented locally, September 1, 2026. No contribution is published.
+The browser user explicitly authorizes extending SafeJS and possibly contributing
+later. SafeJS is the only allowed interpreter dependency; no new dependency may
+be introduced. This checkout is detached at v13.0.10 / 7fbbd81fd99c46928bcf314ad89410b946d203cc.
+
+## Purpose and separation
+
+Keep reusable interpreter capabilities here. Keep HTML, DOM, browser events,
+navigation, network policy, layout and browser clients in the browser package.
+Do not add browser-specific command names, URL policies or site-specific patches
+to SafeJS. Preserve the existing run/replay/snapshot contracts.
+
+The installed SDK remains untouched until an extension is tested and explicitly
+selected by the browser adapter. Use only public exports from the browser; do not
+make it depend on private Scope/interpreter/value implementation paths.
+
+## Extension A: disposable persistent realm
+
+The first implementation exports `createRealm` from the normal and lightweight
+core entrypoints. Twenty-one focused tests pass, including lexical/closure/object
+persistence, no effect replay, lifetime budgets, tracked-promise retention,
+single-flight rejection, cancellation, resource disposal and post-close callback
+revocation. A TypeScript-compiled public-core consumer passes six further checks.
+The installed global runtime and browser integration remain unchanged.
+
+The initial contract deliberately fails closed on every evaluation failure. It
+uses cumulative lifetime budgets and conservatively accounts all submitted source
+and tracked promises until close. It does not implement realm snapshots, module
+imports, guest dynamic code generation, DOM properties or a browser event loop.
+Full-suite validation is not green with the available shared test tooling: missing
+existing upstream dependencies and filesystem type-fixture resolution failures are
+retained in the browser evidence reports. No dependency was installed to mask them.
+This is an experimental contribution candidate, not a security-reviewed release.
+
+- Add an explicit public factory with evaluate and close lifecycle operations.
+- Preserve lexical bindings, object identity and closures across separately parsed
+  source evaluations without replaying prior host effects or concatenating old
+  source each time. Do not implement it by repeatedly restoring a run snapshot
+  with a different source hash.
+- Treat realm access as a trusted-host operation. Do not add guest eval, Function,
+  global host access, dynamic import or ambient modules.
+- Enforce single-flight jobs, cancellation, step/call/data/compilation budgets,
+  retained-source/value accounting and revocation on close. Fatal engine errors
+  remain uncatchable by guests. Old closures and regexes must remain accounted for.
+- Specify which errors leave a usable realm and which dispose it. An interrupted
+  job must not leave an apparently idle realm with continuing host effects.
+- Preserve error identity, promise rejection tracking and cleanup guarantees.
+  Snapshot support for a realm is separate work; reject unsupported capture rather
+  than fabricating a restorable state.
+
+Write failing public-API tests first: persistent state/closure identity; two-realm
+isolation; declaration conflicts; source failure behavior; no effect replay;
+promise jobs; overlap; pending cancellation; retained budgets; close/revocation;
+and guest attempts to suppress fatal budget errors. Existing run tests must pass
+unchanged, including adversarial errors, host callbacks and replay.
+
+## Extension B: explicitly branded host-object capabilities
+
+An initial `createHostObject` implementation is now exported from both public
+entrypoints. Nineteen tests cover explicitly granted live properties/methods,
+identity caches, cycles, nested host arguments, callbacks, asynchronous method
+results, protected members, realm isolation, budgets, disposal and rejection of
+copying/snapshotting live capabilities. The bridge also rejects an ordinary
+`then` accessor before native promise assimilation can invoke it; this negative
+case failed first and now passes. No arbitrary host accessor or proxy is enabled.
+
+Browser-specific DOM bindings remain outside this package. A compiled public-core
+consumer drives the browser's own document tree, including local-only mutations
+of two actually downloaded public pages. This does not enable automatic website
+script execution, event/default-action scheduling or the process protocol.
+
+- Design a public host-object capability with finite explicitly granted members,
+  property reads/writes and method calls, not arbitrary host-object reflection.
+- Support identity-preserving wrapper caching so repeated DOM lookup can return
+  the same guest object. The browser supplies the DOM implementation.
+- All values and arguments cross the existing safe conversion and budgeting
+  boundary. Unknown host prototypes, getters, constructors and functions stay
+  rejected unless represented by the new explicit capability mechanism.
+- Keep capability bookkeeping inaccessible to guest-created structural lookalikes.
+  Cover descriptor/prototype/constructor escape attempts, cycles, aliasing,
+  read-only members, error sanitization and post-close revocation.
+- Make unsupported serialization/replay of live capabilities explicit. Never
+  silently deep-copy a live object and then describe it as a faithful binding.
+
+Write failing tests before implementation for property mutation/read-after-write,
+identity, method receiver behavior, lifecycle and adversarial impostors. Browser
+integration must then verify actual textContent/value/attribute/child mutations
+and cancelable event/default-action ordering; a get/set-shaped custom API alone
+does not satisfy DOM compatibility.
+
+## Extension C: persistent callback phases
+
+`startCallback(callback, args, { thisValue })` accepts only a registered
+persistent-realm guest callback and returns separate `synchronous` and `result`
+promises. The first finishes after the interpreted synchronous body or the async
+body's first suspension; the second follows eventual completion. A synchronous
+function returning a pending promise does not keep its synchronous phase open.
+Explicit receivers retain live-capability identity.
+
+Source evaluations and callback continuations share one realm job queue. External
+overlap during a running prefix is rejected; another source can run while a
+callback is suspended. Host-retained callback identity is stable across calls,
+its captured values remain budgeted, and fatal budget failures revoke the realm.
+Close waits for pending interpreted work to unwind, releases budget/compilation
+leases and revokes further calls. Interrupted host promises retain rejection
+observers so close does not create orphan unhandled rejections.
+
+Fifteen TDD cases cover phases, receiver/argument identity, callback identity,
+retention quotas, rejected overlap, queue ordering, pending close, catch-resistant
+budgets and budget reuse after cancellation. Together with realm, host-object and
+core-export checks, 56 focused tests pass. The broad source run has 7843 passing
+tests, 30 fixture failures and six skips; its 54 failed files are the same as the
+preceding run, including 52 missing-dependency suite-load failures. This is not a
+green upstream release gate; dependencies are not installed to bypass the rule.
+
+The browser's compiled-core probe uses explicit host registration to dispatch
+these callbacks against its live document model, including two read-only public
+pages. It does not provide DOM addEventListener, nested synchronous DOM dispatch,
+full microtask-checkpoint semantics, or a native default-action pipeline. These
+remain integration gates, not properties claimed for this generic SDK API.
+
+## Validation and contribution preparation
+
+### Lightweight page integration exports
+
+The core entrypoint now exports the existing typed `SandboxError` and public
+`deepCopyFromSandbox`. The copy alias moves to a shared module rather than
+duplicating its implementation or exposing internal compilation/live-grant
+unwrapping options. Two new TDD tests verify detached structured results and
+rejection of live grants without evaluating getters. The focused source set now
+has 58 passing tests. The browser's separate owned-session process probe passes
+18 checks using this compiled core and read-only public pages; no DOM, network,
+process supervisor or browser dependency is included in the SafeJS changes.
+The latest broad source run has 7845 passing tests, 30 filesystem type-fixture
+failures and six skips, with the same 54 failed files (52 missing-dependency load
+failures). No dependency was installed and this is not a green release gate.
+
+### Real-site parser regressions
+
+Unmodified jQuery fetched from Quotes to Scrape exposed two generic parser
+failures. Constructor argument lists are now optional, with member/nested-new
+precedence and optional-chain rejection covered. Single statements now consume
+their own optional terminator, fixing unbraced do/while bodies containing
+expressions, declarations, return, break, continue, throw and nested control flow.
+The former if/else-specific semicolon skipping is removed; an unrelated empty
+statement cannot be swallowed to attach an else. This is not complete ASI or
+classic-script grammar conformance.
+
+TDD first reproduced nine constructor failures and thirteen statement-terminator
+failures. The final regressions contain 17 constructor cases and 20 terminator
+cases. All 589 parser tests pass, with one existing opt-in skip. The browser's
+actual compiled-core, read-only diagnostic advances the same public jQuery source
+past both failures and now reports unsupported regex backreferences. No source
+rewriting, native RegExp fallback, budget relaxation or browser dependency is
+introduced. Books to Scrape separately requires document.write; neither site is
+claimed as passing dynamic-site acceptance.
+
+The broad source regression run passes 7882 tests, fails the same 30 filesystem
+type-fixture cases and skips six. Its 54 failed files are identical to the prior
+7845-pass checkpoint, including the same 52 missing-dependency load failures.
+This remains a non-green upstream release gate; no dependencies were installed.
+
+Reference grammar reviewed:
+`https://tc39.es/ecma262/2026/multipage/ecmascript-language-expressions.html#sec-new-operator`.
+
+### Metered regex compatibility
+
+Unmodified public jQuery exposed numeric backreferences and lookahead assertions
+as its next parser blockers. Both now use the existing regex matcher and limits,
+not native RegExp. Backreferences compare code units with per-unit work charging,
+preserve capture/case-folding behavior and support valid forward references.
+Unresolved legacy decimal/octal escapes remain explicitly unsupported. Positive
+lookahead retains only its first successful match and captures without consuming
+input; negative lookahead never exports its captures. Nested assertions retain
+the existing compilation and execution caps.
+
+Thirty-one backreference and thirty-five lookahead tests pass. The regex directory
+passes 190 tests; the broad source run has 7946 passes, 30 fixture failures and six
+skips, including the previously known missing-dependency suite-load failures.
+Its 54 failed file paths are identical to the preceding 7882-pass checkpoint.
+The public jQuery source now parses fully and stops at guest function-property
+assignment. This is progress, not passing website compatibility.
+
+The user authorized upstream issue filing. `poe-platform/poe-code#540` requests a
+supported composable extension/persistent-realm API; #541 requests guest function
+properties and constructor prototypes without exposing native host prototypes.
+Both were verified open. No PR, commit, push, publication or dependency install
+was performed. A proposed extension-contract test patch is stored separately in
+the browser consumer; it is not applied here or counted as implemented.
+
+### Remaining publication gates
+
+September 2 cooperative checkpoint candidate: persistent realms yield every 256
+AST entries without releasing the active guest job. Host timers/cancellation can
+progress while guest callbacks remain queued until their normal turn. Pending
+checkpoint timers and abort listeners are cleaned up. Legacy run cancellation
+and replay semantics remain unchanged. Parsing/native operations/one retained
+graph scan remain non-preemptible, so consumers still need external supervision.
+Failed realm initialization now releases Date roots before Budget reuse.
+
+Fourteen cooperation tests and one additional Date cleanup regression bring the
+full run to 8104 passes, with exactly the prior 30 failed assertions, 54 failed
+files and six skips. The focused run passes 73 tests. Browser probes preserve
+readable public navigation with source timeouts, not completed site JavaScript;
+no production limit is raised. This remains a local candidate, not a release or
+the completed composable extension lifecycle. Performance publication is paused.
+
+September 2 Date candidate: guest timestamp slots and realm-owned prototypes now
+support standard calendar getters/setters, conversion, JSON and explicit clocks.
+Run current-time reads use the existing host-call journal, preserving completed
+reads during replay. Calendar calculations use bounded captured host Date
+primitives without exposing native objects; no dependency is introduced.
+Twenty-two new tests pass. The full run has 8089 passes with the same prior 30
+failed assertions and 54 failed files. The legacy regex checkpoint expectation
+adds only the new default Date binding; hashes and graph checks remain exact.
+Date object snapshots/copies and locale formatting explicitly reject. They are
+not claimed complete, nor is the extension lifecycle. Browser diagnostics advance
+past Date, but both public production navigations still fail the existing
+heartbeat. No upstream publication is made in this checkpoint.
+
+- Existing scope baseline: 28 tests passed using the already-installed repository
+  test tooling. The checkout's node_modules link is test-only; no installation or
+  manifest/lockfile change occurred.
+- Run focused public-API tests, relevant interpreter/bridge/budget/replay tests,
+  then the full SafeJS suite and public-export consumer checks.
+- Keep patches self-contained, document intentional gaps, and carry no browser
+  implementation or additional dependency into this package.
+- Do not commit, push, open issues/PRs or publish without a separate instruction.
+- Do not alter the installed global package in place. The browser should select
+  a tested build explicitly and record its source/version.
